WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.892
Model size creates a tradeoff among capability, speed, memory, cost, and deployment complexity.

00:00:05.892 --> 00:00:13.197
A small or distilled model may run comfortably on a laptop or modest GPU and can be excellent

00:00:13.197 --> 00:00:16.357
for teaching, prototyping, and high-volume classification.

00:00:16.357 --> 00:00:24.537
A medium model often gives a useful balance between performance and operational cost.

00:00:24.537 --> 00:00:29.272
A large model may handle more complex generation and reasoning tasks, but it requires more memory,

00:00:29.272 --> 00:00:36.197
longer inference time, and often higher financial or infrastructure cost.

00:00:36.197 --> 00:00:38.907
Bigger is not automatically better.

00:00:38.907 --> 00:00:45.082
If a small encoder reaches the required classification accuracy with ten times lower latency,

00:00:45.082 --> 00:00:48.822
using a much larger chat model may be unnecessary.

00:00:48.822 --> 00:00:55.227
Model selection should be driven by the minimum capability needed to satisfy the task reliably.

00:00:55.227 --> 00:01:01.717
Benchmark candidate models on your own data and measure not only quality but also runtime, memory use,

00:01:01.717 --> 00:01:03.440
and cost.
